WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.930
JENNY BROWN: In a
previous episode,

00:00:01.930 --> 00:00:03.990
we talked about how
Critter Junction acquired

00:00:03.990 --> 00:00:05.790
another company and
had to integrate

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:08.940
their VMs with their
existing architecture.

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:12.270
This had gone well so
far, but Critter Junction

00:00:12.270 --> 00:00:13.740
had noticed some
areas where things

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.620
could be improved using
Google Cloud Persistent

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:20.400
Disks to save money and to make
the entire application more

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:21.390
scalable.

00:00:21.390 --> 00:00:23.335
Stay tuned to see how.

00:00:23.335 --> 00:00:27.135
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:32.740
After the big acquisition,
Critter Junction

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:37.090
had to maintain and, ideally,
upgrade another company's VMs.

00:00:37.090 --> 00:00:39.700
After using the architecture
for a few months,

00:00:39.700 --> 00:00:42.610
one of the first potential areas
of improvement Critter Junction

00:00:42.610 --> 00:00:45.430
found was that there were
many of these VMs disks that

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:47.710
were being
underutilized, meaning

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:50.110
the speed at which the
application was requesting

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:53.110
data was much slower than
the speed their disk could

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:54.310
read the data.

00:00:54.310 --> 00:00:56.980
And they realized that other
parts of the architecture

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:59.500
were overutilizing resources.

00:00:59.500 --> 00:01:01.300
So Critter Junction
also realized

00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:03.490
if their growth
projections were accurate,

00:01:03.490 --> 00:01:05.349
they would need to
resize their VMs'

00:01:05.349 --> 00:01:08.620
disks as they would very quickly
outgrow their current disk

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:09.820
size.

00:01:09.820 --> 00:01:13.030
And on top of all that,
they wanted this resizing

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:16.840
to happen without any downtime
or costly VM restarts,

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:18.560
if possible.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:21.520
This seemed like a lot to ask
for, but what they didn't know

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:23.470
is that Google Cloud
Persistent Disks

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:25.360
solve a lot of their problems.

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:26.650
Let's dig in.

00:01:26.650 --> 00:01:28.510
Google Cloud
Persistent Disks are

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:31.150
a durable, high-performance
block-storage option

00:01:31.150 --> 00:01:32.980
for Cloud VMs.

00:01:32.980 --> 00:01:35.920
Their main benefit is that
they can be attached to

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:40.450
or detached from VMs as needed,
allowing for long-lived data

00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:43.090
to persist even where
those VMs are started,

00:01:43.090 --> 00:01:45.460
stopped, or terminated.

00:01:45.460 --> 00:01:48.640
There are three different
types of block storage.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.970
Local SSDs are
high-performance scratch spaces

00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:54.700
that are best suited for
in-memory databases up

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:57.040
to 9 terabytes in size.

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.740
Then there's standard
persistent disks.

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:02.980
These are network devices
suited for large data-processing

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:06.730
workloads that primarily
use sequential IOs.

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:09.160
There are SSD
persistent disks, which

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:11.290
are suited for
enterprise applications

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:13.690
and high-performance
database needs that

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:16.210
require lower
latency and more IOPs

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:19.090
than standard persistent
disks provide.

00:02:19.090 --> 00:02:21.160
SSD persistent
disks are designed

00:02:21.160 --> 00:02:23.620
for single-digit-millisecond
latencies.

00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:26.560
The observed latency
is app specific.

00:02:26.560 --> 00:02:29.560
And finally, there are
balanced persistent disks

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:32.890
which, as named, provide
a balance between PD

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:35.560
standard and PD SSDs.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:39.250
They are backed by a solid-state
drives that balance performance

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:41.110
and cost.

00:02:41.110 --> 00:02:43.610
Now that we reviewed
the different types,

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:46.390
let's see how this applied
to Critter Junction.

00:02:46.390 --> 00:02:50.320
Critter Junction mostly uses
standard persistent disks,

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:54.490
which are good for using as
boot devices, persisting data,

00:02:54.490 --> 00:02:57.550
used as data-distribution
devices for static website

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:00.130
content, and they
can be a useful way

00:03:00.130 --> 00:03:02.860
to replicate data across zones.

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:05.110
Unfortunately, the
current architecture

00:03:05.110 --> 00:03:07.240
didn't take much advantage
of these features

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:11.080
because each PD is attached
to a corresponding VM.

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:13.450
As we mentioned, this
caused the application

00:03:13.450 --> 00:03:17.770
to underutilized some PDs
while overutilizing others.

00:03:17.770 --> 00:03:19.840
Another concern
Critter Junction had

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:23.410
is that as their application
data and boot volume grows,

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:27.010
they're afraid they'll have to
reboot the VM, detach the PD,

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:30.400
resize it, and reattach
it, resulting in scaling

00:03:30.400 --> 00:03:32.710
limits with downtime.

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:35.290
Fortunately, this
isn't the case at all.

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:36.940
What Critter
Junction didn't know

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:39.430
is that persistent disks
provide scalability

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:41.740
because multiple VM
instances can connect

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:45.880
to content at the same time
within the same zone, allowing

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:49.630
applications to scale up
or instances down as needed

00:03:49.630 --> 00:03:51.640
to serve data to customers.

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:54.220
And better yet, attaching
a persistent disk

00:03:54.220 --> 00:03:56.170
to multiple virtual
machines doesn't

00:03:56.170 --> 00:03:59.320
affect total performance
or cost either.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:03.130
Each machine gets a share of
the per disk performance limit.

00:04:03.130 --> 00:04:05.710
Performance scales
automatically with size,

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:10.300
so you can resize existing PDs
or add more PDs to an instance

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:13.630
to meet performance and
storage-space requirements.

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:17.079
Each PD can be up to
64 terabytes in size,

00:04:17.079 --> 00:04:19.300
so there's no need to
manage arrays of disk

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:22.300
to create large logical volumes.

00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:25.660
Critter Junction also didn't
realize that persistent disks

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:28.120
provide nearly
unlimited flexibility

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:31.030
by allowing you to resize
your storage while it's

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:36.520
in use by one or more virtual
machines with no downtime.

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:39.460
That means they could resize
their disks on the fly

00:04:39.460 --> 00:04:41.960
without interruption.

00:04:41.960 --> 00:04:44.890
This is all made possible
by Google Cloud Persistent

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:46.840
Disks being network attached.

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:48.610
Since the disks
aren't physically

00:04:48.610 --> 00:04:50.680
attached to the VMs
that are using them,

00:04:50.680 --> 00:04:53.830
you get the benefit of using
Google's network fabric, which

00:04:53.830 --> 00:04:57.550
can deliver more than 1 petabit
per second of total bisection

00:04:57.550 --> 00:04:58.880
bandwidth.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:00.550
That's a lot of that.

00:05:00.550 --> 00:05:02.440
With all of this
new information,

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:04.450
Critter Junction went
ahead and moved away

00:05:04.450 --> 00:05:07.330
from assigning one PD per VM.

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:10.060
This allowed them to
connect more VM instances

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:12.220
to their underutilized disks.

00:05:12.220 --> 00:05:15.370
These instances all read from
the same standard persistent

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:17.920
disk, allowing the
entire application

00:05:17.920 --> 00:05:19.810
to serve more users.

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:22.330
And for the disks that
were overutilized,

00:05:22.330 --> 00:05:25.780
Critter Junction decided to
upgrade them to larger disks.

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:27.490
And best of all,
Critter Junction

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:30.280
took advantage of Google Cloud
Persistent Disks' ability

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:33.550
to resize a storage
while it's in use by a VM

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:36.610
to do all of this
without any downtime.

00:05:36.610 --> 00:05:39.310
For Critter Junction, Google
Cloud Persistent Disks

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:42.490
turn out to be a simple,
fast, and flexible solution

00:05:42.490 --> 00:05:44.360
for those needs.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:47.590
So that's a wrap for this
episode of "Season of Scale."

00:05:47.590 --> 00:05:49.930
Stay tuned for more
about Critter Junction.

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:52.180
And remember, always
be architecting.

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:55.830
[MUSIC PLAYING]

